# Working tree is ~34 GB; the source in it is ~3 MB. Everything below is either
# a build output, a flight artefact, someone else's repository, or a file this
# repository can regenerate. Sizes are measured, not estimated.

# --- Simulators and engine builds ------------------------------------------
# PASBlocks is the Unreal project and its build output.
PASBlocks/
# Unrelated projects that happen to share this folder. Never ours to commit.
USSCVNSupercarrier/
USSCVNSupercarrier*/
DronePack/
airsim_lab/

# --- Other people's repositories --------------------------------------------
# kuanting-vla-uav-guardrail is Prof. Lai's repo with its own .git; source/
# holds read-only clones (AeroVLA, uav-vla, UAV-VLPA, vla-survey).
kuanting-vla-uav-guardrail/
source/

# --- Flight artefacts -------------------------------------------------------
# 2.9 GB across 18,138 files, of which 16,442 are recorded JPEG frames. Every
# flight rewrites these; metrics that matter are quoted into docs/data/.
# NOTE the trailing /* rather than a plain `demo/out/`. Git cannot re-include a
# file whose PARENT DIRECTORY is excluded, so `demo/out/` followed by
# `!demo/out/citymap/` silently keeps the exclusion and the negation never runs.
# That is exactly what happened here: the occupancy grids were described in this
# file as kept, and `git check-ignore` reported them still matched by
# `demo/out/`. Excluding the ENTRIES lets the negation below take effect.
demo/out/*
logs/
_archive/

# ...except the occupancy grids. They live under demo/out/ by accident of where
# build_voxel_map.py writes them, but they are INPUT to the Shield's obstacle
# clearance rule, not output of a flight. 7 KB each, and rebuilding them needs
# the simulator, so a fresh clone that lacks them cannot fly at all.
!demo/out/citymap/

# Experiment output, same category: 299 files and 34 MB, which would be 87% of
# this repository. Docs reference it as a raw-data pointer, exactly as they do
# demo/out/. A figure that a document actually needs is copied into docs/img/,
# which is tracked - that is the existing convention.
experiments/out/

# --- Datasets and weights ---------------------------------------------------
# Standing rule: no models, 3D maps or datasets in git.
dataset/
training/data/
models/*.pt
models/*.onnx
models/*.safetensors
*.glb
*.fbx
*.uasset

# --- Runtime and sync litter ------------------------------------------------
projectairsim_client.log
OneDrive_*.zip
.claude/settings.local.json

# --- Generated documents ----------------------------------------------------
# All of these are rebuilt from tracked sources:
#   docs/MIDTERM-REPORT-Aug2026.md  -> tools/report_to_html.py -> Word -> .docx/.pdf
#   tools/deck/build_midterm_deck.js -> .pptx -> PowerPoint -> .pdf
# Tracking them would put a 486 MB deck in the object store permanently.
docs/*.pptx
docs/*.pdf
docs/*.docx
docs/*.html
docs/video/
demo/**/*.mp4

# --- Python -----------------------------------------------------------------
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.venv/
venv/

# --- Node (deck build) ------------------------------------------------------
node_modules/
package-lock.json

# --- Editors and OS ---------------------------------------------------------
.vscode/
.idea/
.zcode/
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
*.swp
# POLICY bundles are byte-derivable from the tracked YAML, so ignoring them
# costs nothing - regenerate with python -m guardrail.bundle.
bundles/*.tar.gz
# REPLAY bundles are not. They wrap a flight log, and demo/out/ is ignored, so a
# replay bundle cannot be rebuilt from a clone. WP4 names it as a deliverable;
# ignoring it left the artefact existing in exactly one working tree. They are
# ~100 KiB each, which is what an episode compresses to, and they are kept.
!bundles/*.replay.tar.gz

# INTERNAL RECORDS, deliberately not published.
#
# This repository is public. `meeting notes/` names colleagues and records what
# was said in project meetings, and CORRECTIONS-2026-09-02.md is a list of five
# published numbers that need correcting - written to be sent to those people
# first, not read by them on the internet.
#
# Removed from the whole history at first publication (2026-09-10), not merely
# from HEAD: `git rm --cached` would have left them readable in every earlier
# commit. The local files are still on disk; they are simply not tracked.
meeting notes/
docs/CORRECTIONS-2026-09-02.md
